  "question": "What has been proposed to reduce the risk of a reinforcement-learning agent damaging task-irrelevant parts of its environment, and by what mechanism?",
  "reference_answer": "One proposed intervention is a training-time impact penalty that uses the environment’s initial state as a baseline. The agent is charged an amount ϵ whenever a cell changes away from its initial value and credited ϵ if it restores that value, excluding cells containing the agent or its goals. Consequently, disturbing unrelated surroundings lowers the agent’s return, while preserving or restoring them avoids that cost. This adds an incentive for environmental preservation alongside task completion. The intervention has limited effectiveness: ϵ=1.0 provided a performance–safety trade-off in the tested creation and removal tasks, but safety remained poor, and larger penalties encouraged inaction. The initial-state comparison is also not expected to work well in dynamic environments.",
  "spans": [
    "Along with the naively unsafe agents, we train agents with a very simple side effect impact penalty using a starting-state baseline. We penalize the agent an amount ϵ for every cell that deviates from its starting state,",
    "The agent receives the penalty only when a cell changes from its starting value. Conversely, the agent is credited an amount ϵ if it resets a cell to its initial value.",
    "excepting cells that contain the agent itself or its goals. Though straightforward and easy to calculate, we do not expect this to perform well in dynamic settings.",
    "A value of ϵ=1.0 seems to provide a good trade-off between performance and safety for each of the two tasks, although the safety performance is still quite poor. With larger penalties the agents tend towards inaction and never complete their goals."
  ]
}
